[38;2;124;124;124mAnd the key computation $B(i\theta T\psi, i\theta T\psi) = |i\theta|^2 B(T\psi, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mT\psi) = \theta^2 Q(T\psi)$ holds as long as $B$ is sesquilinear in the standard[0m[38;2;124;124;124msense.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf $B \equiv 0$, then $Q \equiv 0$ and $Q(T\psi) = 0$ trivially. So the answer [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mis yes in all cases.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me just present the clean proof.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me reconsider the problem statement once more. It says $B: H [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\times H \to \mathbb{R}$ is a "continuous sesquilinear form." In many contexts, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mespecially in PDE theory and physics, a "sesquilinear form" on a complex Hilbert[0m[38;2;124;124;124mspace that is "real-valued" is understood to be a Hermitian form — one where [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$B(\phi, \psi) = \overline{B(\psi, \phi)}$. The real-valuedness is a property [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(Hermitian symmetry) rather than a constraint on the codomain for all inputs. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mThe notation $B: H \times H \to \mathbb{R}$ might be shorthand for "the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124massociated quadratic form is real-valued."[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIn any case, the proof works. Let me write it up.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHmm, but actually there's another possible interpretation: maybe $B$ is only $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathbb{R}$-bilinear (or $\mathbb{R}$-sesquilinear in some weaker sense). But [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe standard meaning of "sesquilinear form on a complex Hilbert space" is clear:[0m[38;2;124;124;124mlinear in one argument, conjugate-linear in the other.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mI think the cleanest interpretation is: $B$ is a (complex) sesquilinear form [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthat is Hermitian ($B(\phi,\psi) = \overline{B(\psi,\phi)}$), hence $Q(\psi) = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mB(\psi,\psi) \in \mathbb{R}$. The notation $B: H \times H \to \mathbb{R}$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124memphasizes that $Q$ is real-valued.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mOK let me write the proof.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mProof:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince $\psi \in \text{dom}(T)$, by definition of the generator of the unitary [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgroup:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\lim_{\theta \to 0} \frac{e^{i\theta T}\psi - \psi}{\theta} = iT\psi \quad [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\text{in norm}.$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mDefine $r_\theta = e^{i\theta T}\psi - \psi - i\theta T\psi$, so $\|r_\theta\| =[0m[38;2;124;124;124mo(|\theta|)$ as $\theta \to 0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSet $\phi_\theta = e^{i\theta T}\psi - \psi = i\theta T\psi + r_\theta$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince $B$ is continuous (bounded), there exists $C > 0$ such that $|B(u, v)| [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\leq C\|u\|\|v\|$ for all $u, v \in H$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mExpand:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$Q(\phi_\theta) = B(\phi_\theta, \phi_\theta) = B(i\theta T\psi + r_\theta, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mi\theta T\psi + r_\theta)$$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$= B(i\theta T\psi, i\theta T\psi) + B(i\theta T\psi, r_\theta) + B(r_\theta, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mi\theta T\psi) + B(r_\theta, r_\theta).$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**First term:** By sesquilinearity, $B(i\theta T\psi, i\theta T\psi) = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(i\theta)\overline{(i\theta)} B(T\psi, T\psi) = \theta^2 Q(T\psi)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m(Here we use $|i\theta|^2 = \theta^2$; this holds regardless of which argument [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mis linear vs. conjugate-linear.)[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Cross terms:** By boundedness of $B$:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$|B(i\theta T\psi, r_\theta)| \leq C|\theta|\|T\psi\|\|r_\theta\| = C|\theta|\|[0m[38;2;124;124;124mT\psi\| \cdot o(|\theta|) = o(\theta^2),$$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$|B(r_\theta, i\theta T\psi)| \leq C\|r_\theta\||\theta|\|T\psi\| = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mo(\theta^2).$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Last term:**[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$|B(r_\theta, r_\theta)| \leq C\|r_\theta\|^2 = o(\theta^2).$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mCombining:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$Q(\phi_\theta) = \theta^2 Q(T\psi) + o(\theta^2).$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe hypothesis states $Q(\phi_\theta) = O(|\theta|^3)$, i.e., $|Q(\phi_\theta)| [0m[38;2;124;124;124m\leq M|\theta|^3$ for small $\theta$. In particular, $Q(\phi_\theta) = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mo(\theta^2)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\theta^2 Q(T\psi) = Q(\phi_\theta) - o(\theta^2) = o(\theta^2) - o(\theta^2) =[0m[38;2;124;124;124mo(\theta^2).$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mDividing by $\theta^2$ (for $\theta \neq 0$):[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$Q(T\psi) = o(1) \to 0 \quad \text{as } \theta \to 0.$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince $Q(T\psi)$ is a fixed real number independent of $\theta$, we conclude:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$Q(T\psi) = 0.$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$\blacksquare$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe answer is **yes**.[0m

