$Q(e^{i\theta T}\psi - \psi)$ is exactly $\theta^2 Q(T\psi)$, arising from the
first-order term $i\theta T\psi$ in the expansion of $e^{i\theta T}\psi$. The
hypothesis that the quadratic form vanishes to third order forces this second-
order term to be zero, yielding $Q(T\psi) = 0$. No assumption on $\psi \in
\operatorname{dom}(T^2)$ is needed; the first-order expansion suffices because
$B$ is bounded.


